# Part of the harness asset library c6f3b4427d58f7c3 (see VERSION.json) · 2026-09-14
#
# checks.env — the one file a spoke owns that the hub reads.
#
# Flat KEY=value, one per line, no quoting, no shell. This is the only home for
# this repository's commands and coverage numbers. The hub's list records where
# this file is; it never copies what is in it. /dev-sprint reads this file from
# the repository's trunk before it dispatches anyone, and runs exactly these.
#
# Three states for a value, and only three:
#   a command        run it; a non-zero exit is a failed check
#   none             this repository has no such step, said on purpose.
#                    `none — <why>` gives the reason, printed wherever the
#                    row is reported
#   UNRESOLVED       no working command yet; every command that needs the key
#                    stops with exit 2. "Not configured" never means "fine".
#                    Bare, it means nobody has chosen one. `UNRESOLVED — <reason>.
#                    Owner: <role>. Resolves: <trigger>.` says why: unclear
#                    evidence, or a command found wrong at the install
# A missing key is the same as UNRESOLVED. An empty value is a stop.
#
# Drafted by /add-repo from the evidence it found; settled at the spoke install,
# where every command below is run once on this repository's trunk. A wrong
# command fails there, not in a sprint.

# How a new checkout of this repository gets its dependencies. The harness
# runs this command once, through /bin/sh at the repository root, in each new
# checkout it makes for a sprint: the code checkout before the test writer
# starts, the engineer's clone, and the finalizer's checkout of every round,
# where it is the first row. The story-gate coverage step runs it too, before it
# measures. The commands below then use what it installed. A
# new checkout holds only tracked files. Without this step a command finds no
# tool, or `npx` fetches the newest version of one instead of the pinned one.
#
# It is judged like the other rows: by its exit code and by the tree. It may
# write files git ignores (node_modules/, vendor/, .venv/). A command that
# changes a tracked file fails, so it must not rewrite the lockfile: write
# `npm ci`, not `npm install`. Where the build does not need the install
# scripts of the packages, stop them (`npm ci --ignore-scripts`). Write the
# install step this repository's CI already runs. `none — <why>` says on
# purpose that a checkout needs nothing installed, for example a repository
# that uses only the standard library. UNRESOLVED stops /dev-sprint before
# anyone is dispatched.
INSTALL_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED

# The first three are the commit checks: every harness commit in this
# repository runs them on what is staged (scripts/check-staged.mjs in the hub,
# through scripts/harness-commit.mjs), reading this file from the trunk, never
# from the branch. A row is judged by its exit code and by the tree: a command
# that changes a file fails, so a formatter may rewrite in place or only check.
# Each commit records the rows as `Harness-Check:` trailers above the watermark.
# A trunk that fails one of the three stops the spoke install.

FORMAT_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED
LINT_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED
TYPECHECK_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED
BUILD_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED
TEST_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED

# The paths whose approved contents decide what the tests are: test sources --
# in test folders and beside the code (`**/*.test.ts`, `**/*_test.go`) --
# fixtures, snapshots, test helpers, and every file that configures test
# discovery or the runner -- plus this file itself, so a change to the test
# command or to this list never rides along in a code pull request. In
# /dev-sprint, once the critic approves the test writer's commits, every tracked
# entry these patterns match is frozen, and only the test writer adds or changes
# a test. An engineer's edit, deletion, rename, replacement or mode change of
# one, or a new path they match -- a new test included -- is refused in the
# agent, put back or moved aside after a shell command, and undone in the one
# commit the finalizer builds before anything is accepted (scripts/test-guard.mjs
# in the hub) -- it never counts. /dev-sprint verifies that the approved tests
# are unchanged and that each command exits 0; it does not verify that every
# approved test ran. A path these patterns
# do not match is production code the engineer may change, stubs included, so a
# stub never sits under a protected pattern.
#
# Grammar: comma-separated patterns, each matched against the whole path from
# the repository root. `**` is a whole segment (any number of folders; a
# trailing `/**` is everything inside a folder), `*` any run of characters in
# one segment, `?` one character. No negation, no braces, no brackets, no
# absolute path, no `..`. Name a file a runner would read even where it does
# not exist yet (pytest.ini, **/conftest.py): adding one is then caught too.
#
# What the patterns must cover. Setup and `check` refuse a tracked file they
# leave uncovered when it is a runner configuration (jest.config.*,
# vitest.config.*, .mocharc*, pytest.ini, conftest.py ...); a file a supported
# runner collects as a test by default (*.test.* and *.spec.*, *.cy.*,
# *_test.go, test_*.py, *_spec.rb, *.snap, __tests__/, __mocks__/,
# __snapshots__/, __fixtures__/, test/ or tests/ at the root, src/test/); or a
# mixed file that configures a runner -- a package.json with a top-level jest,
# mocha, ava, nyc, c8 or tap key, a pyproject.toml [tool.pytest...] or
# [tool.coverage...] table, a setup.cfg [tool:pytest] or [coverage:...]
# section. The guard protects files whole and reads no field inside one: list
# such a file here whole, which freezes its dependencies for the sprint too, or
# move the runner settings into a file of their own and list that. A test
# command that runs vitest with no --config and no vitest.config.* needs its
# vite.config.* listed, for the same reason, and a new vite.config.* there is
# refused during a sprint, because vitest would load it; anywhere else a
# vite.config.* is the build's and stays free. During a sprint a new file of those kinds is
# refused anywhere, and runner configuration added to a package.json,
# pyproject.toml or setup.cfg left free is undone; any other change to one is
# production code. Protection is these patterns plus those supported
# conventions, never a reading of what code does: a runner configured to collect
# other names needs them listed here, and test code that follows no convention
# outside these patterns is not seen.
#
# The test command must not change unless a protected file changes: a runner
# script or configuration file TEST_COMMAND names is listed here, and an alias
# (`make test`, `just test`) has its controlling file listed. An alias defined in
# a file that also holds dependencies (`npm test` and package.json, gradle, mvn,
# composer) is refused: write the runner directly (`node --test ...`,
# `npx jest ...`, `pytest ...`). `none` or UNRESOLVED stops /dev-sprint.
# UNRESOLVED — which paths hold this repository's tests and their
# configuration. Owner: tech lead. Resolves: at this repository's spoke install,
# proved with `node <hub>/scripts/test-guard.mjs check --repo .`.
TEST_GUARD_PATHS=UNRESOLVED

# This repository's own secret scan. Mandatory: a committed secret stays in the
# history, so it is caught before the commit exists. The hook .githooks/pre-commit
# runs it on the staged changes, harness-commit.mjs runs it before every commit
# the harness makes here (as a row of check-staged.mjs, beside format, lint and
# type check), and story-gate runs it over every commit of every pull request.
# Missing, empty, UNRESOLVED and `none` are all refused: the spoke install
# stops, story-gate fails, every commit is refused. UNRESOLVED and a missing key
# are exit 2, cannot verify, as for the other commit checks; `none` is exit 1,
# because a spoke has no choice to make here.
#
# Like the other commit checks, the command is read from the trunk, never from
# the branch being committed: the head of origin's default branch for a commit,
# and the base of the pull request for story-gate. A branch that edits this line
# changes nothing until a person merges it. The install's own pull request is the
# one exception, because no trunk has the file yet: the command is read from the
# pull request itself, where a person reads it before they merge.
#
# The command is this repository's, never the harness's. It must run unchanged
# on Windows, Linux and Mac, in a clean checkout where only git and Node are
# present, through the platform's shell (sh, or cmd.exe on Windows): a
# package.json script or `node <script>` does; make, bash and Python tools do not.
# Anything else it needs, it fetches itself. It reads what to scan from
#   SHL_SCAN_MODE=staged                    the index, before a commit
#   SHL_SCAN_MODE=range SHL_SCAN_RANGE=<revisions>
#                                           every commit `git log <revisions>`
#                                           lists: <base>..<head> for a pull
#                                           request, HEAD for the whole history
# and exits 0 when clean.
# UNRESOLVED — which scanner this repository uses. Owner: tech lead. Resolves:
# before this repository's spoke install, which refuses until it is a command.
SECRET_SCAN_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED

# One more step the pre-merge check reports on. This, and RULES_INDEX and
# CONTRACTS_INDEX below, are the exception to "UNRESOLVED stops the run": while
# one is UNRESOLVED the pre-merge check reports that row NOT CONFIGURED in every
# pull-request body, which is never a pass and never a stop. A command that
# finds something, once one exists, refuses the pull request. `none` says on
# purpose that this repository has no migrations.
MIGRATION_IMMUTABILITY_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED

# How this repository measures coverage, and the two floors decision 2a names:
# whole-project line coverage must not fall below PROJECT_COVERAGE_FLOOR, and the
# lines a change adds or modifies must clear CHANGED_LINE_FLOOR. This file is the
# single home of both numbers. The project floor is measured on this
# repository's trunk at the spoke install and written rounded down; it is raised
# by hand between sprints, to a level the trunk has held, and never falls. The
# changed-line floor is the fixed percentage decision 2a sets. Never carried in
# from another repository.
#
# The command's contract. The harness ships no coverage tool: name your own. It
# runs once through /bin/sh at the repository root, with HARNESS_COVERAGE_BASE
# (the merge base) and HARNESS_COVERAGE_HEAD (the commit measured; in /dev-sprint
# the candidate's tree, committed once the checks pass: either way, something
# `git diff` takes) set, and prints on stdout:
#   project=<number>   whole-project line coverage, 0 to 100
#   changed=<number>   line coverage of the lines added or changed between the two
#   changed=n/a        it measured, and no measurable line changed
# A single number alone on its last line is read as project=. A command that
# prints no changed= line cannot be judged against CHANGED_LINE_FLOOR, and that
# is a stop, never a pass. A server-side tool (SonarQube's coverage and
# new_coverage measures, say) is read by a small script that prints the two
# lines; the sample estate's sample-service/scripts/coverage.mjs is a worked
# example over Node's own coverage.
#
# Where it is judged, by scripts/check-coverage.mjs in the hub: the story-gate
# check runs it on every pull request, reading the command and both floors from
# this file on the trunk, never from the branch; /dev-sprint's finalizer runs it
# on every round, from the copy frozen at setup. Every result records the bar it
# met: `project 72.5% (floor 70) · changed 96% (floor 95) · floors from
# checks.env@<12 hex>`, the hex naming these bytes.
#
# Unlike SECRET_SCAN_COMMAND, MIGRATION_IMMUTABILITY_COMMAND and RULES_INDEX,
# which report NOT CONFIGURED, UNRESOLVED here is a stop: while COVERAGE_COMMAND,
# or either floor beside a command, is UNRESOLVED, the story-gate check cannot
# verify (exit 2), every gated pull request is red, and /dev-sprint will not
# start. `none` on COVERAGE_COMMAND says on purpose that this repository has no
# coverage step; a numeric floor beside it is refused.
COVERAGE_COMMAND=UNRESOLVED
PROJECT_COVERAGE_FLOOR=UNRESOLVED
CHANGED_LINE_FLOOR=UNRESOLVED

# The folder of this repository's own rules: how code here is built, named,
# structured and merged, in prose only where no tool can check it. The hub
# decides whether and what; this folder decides how. Flat, `.md` only, every
# file opening with the frontmatter the hub's rule pack uses (`name`,
# `description`, `globs`, `alwaysApply`), exactly one file `alwaysApply: true`,
# every file within 80 lines. A file may add `overrides`: the hub or global
# standards, whole or one rule, that it replaces where it binds; the merge here
# is the approval, and the hub proves each id when it reads this repository.
# The hub reads it through
# `scripts/read-spoke-rules.mjs` at a pinned commit, keeps the always-on rule
# plus those whose globs match the paths a design touches, and hands that text
# to the design author, the worker and the critic. Nothing loads a rule by glob;
# the script selects by glob. A convention a formatter or linter can enforce
# belongs in a command above, not in a rule file. `none` says on purpose that
# this repository has no written rules. UNRESOLVED or missing reports NOT
# CONFIGURED in every pull-request body and every design, and never stops a run.
# The story-gate check runs the hub's rules checker against this checkout on
# every pull request while a folder is declared here.
# `/add-context --kind rule --repo <this id>`, run in the hub, drafts one rule
# into this folder from the hub's rule template and opens a pull request here.
# UNRESOLVED — whether this repository writes rules, and where. Owner: tech
# lead. Resolves: at this repository's spoke install.
RULES_INDEX=UNRESOLVED

# The folder of this repository's contracts: the versioned interfaces it consumes
# or serves, and the documentation of any third-party API it calls, copied in
# unchanged. Every `.md` file in it opens with the frontmatter the hub's rule
# pack uses (`name`, `description`, `globs`, `alwaysApply`) plus `kind`
# (`contract` or `vendor-doc`), `owner` (a role), `revision` (of the original)
# and `origin` (where the copy came from). A machine form -- an OpenAPI file, a
# schema -- sits beside the prose and is not a document. The hub reads the
# folder through `scripts/read-context.mjs` at a pinned commit, selects the
# documents a design names, whose globs match a path it touches, or that are
# always on, and hands that text to the design author, the worker and the
# critic; `scripts/check-context.mjs` proves the folder parses.
# `none` says on purpose that this repository has no contracts. UNRESOLVED or
# missing reports NOT CONFIGURED in every run and every design, never a stop,
# and a contracts/ folder on disk is not read until this key says so.
# UNRESOLVED — whether this repository keeps contracts, and where. Owner: tech
# lead. Resolves: at this repository's spoke install.
CONTRACTS_INDEX=UNRESOLVED

# The folder of this repository's own decision records: the decisions that bind
# only this repository's code, kept here so that every hub of this repository
# reads one copy. Each file is `ADR-<NNN>-<slug>.md`, numbered by this repository,
# with the header a hub's records carry, `status` included. Everywhere else its
# id is `<this repository's id>.ADR-<NNN>`, so a bare ADR-<NNN> is always a hub's.
# A record here narrows the hub's records and never overrides one. The hub
# reads the folder as it reads CONTRACTS_INDEX: through scripts/read-context.mjs,
# at a pinned commit. `/add-context --kind adr --repo <this id>` writes one.
# `none` says on purpose that this repository keeps no records of its own.
# UNRESOLVED or missing reports NOT CONFIGURED, never a stop, and a decisions/
# folder on disk is not read until this key says so.
# UNRESOLVED — whether this repository keeps decision records of its own, and
# where. Owner: tech lead. Resolves: at this repository's spoke install.
DECISIONS_INDEX=UNRESOLVED

# The required check on this repository's code pull requests clones the hub and
# runs the pickup gate there for the branch's work-item key. A branch that names
# no key fails that check, unless it matches the one pattern recorded here.
# The spoke install writes `^harness/`: the install pull request itself, and
# every later harness-maintenance pull request, is not a story and merges
# without a design. That is the whole of the exception this library writes.
# `none` means no exception. Anything wider -- a hotfix path that merges
# without a design -- is the owner's decision, written here with a reason.
# UNRESOLVED — whether any exception beyond `^harness/` is allowed. Owner:
# engineering lead. Resolves: at this repository's spoke install.
STORY_GATE_EXCEPTION=^harness/

# The hubs this repository belongs to: one entry per hub, `<PREFIX> <owner>/<repo>`,
# entries joined by `, `. PREFIX is the prefix of the keys that hub mints, the
# `prefix` in its docs/backlog/keys.json. owner/repo is the hub as this host
# names it. The required check reads the prefix of a branch's key and clones only
# the hub with that prefix. One prefix names one hub. Every hub listed runs the
# same major version of the library. The spoke install writes the entry of the
# hub it runs from, and adds one entry when a second hub installs this
# repository. Missing, empty, UNRESOLVED or malformed is exit 2 on every pull
# request: the check cannot know which hub to ask.
HUBS=UNRESOLVED

# The library this repository's harness files came from: `<version> <commit>`,
# the harnessVersion and the full library commit. This is the spoke's own
# receipt, so no hub owns the spoke's version: the spoke install writes it, and
# every upgrade from any hub in HUBS rewrites it. An upgrade reads it before its
# own hub's receipt. It leaves a spoke already newer than its library as it is.
# It refuses a new major version while another hub in HUBS runs the old one.
# Never edit it by hand.
HARNESS_LIBRARY=UNRESOLVED

# The branch the harness treats as this repository's trunk. Required. Write
# `default` to use the branch the remote reports as its default. Write a branch
# name where another team keeps the default branch, and the harness team works
# on a second, protected branch (the guide's "A spoke that two teams share").
# The branch must exist on the remote. The value must be the same as `"trunk"`
# in this repository's entry in the registry of every hub in HUBS, or every
# harness check stops with exit 2. One repository has one harness trunk. The value states a fact about the repository, so it stays the
# same on both branches after a merge. A missing or empty value is exit 2.
TRUNK_FOR_HUB=default

# The branches that carry the default branch into a declared trunk (a sync).
# Required. `none` where TRUNK_FOR_HUB is `default`, because there is nothing to
# sync. For a declared trunk the spoke install writes `^sync/`, an extended
# regular expression. On a sync, story-gate runs only the secret scan: the
# design gate, the coverage floors, the watermark step and the rule-file check
# do not run. A person reviews the merge. A branch that names a work item is
# always a story, whatever this pattern matches. A branch that matches this
# pattern and STORY_GATE_EXCEPTION is exit 2. A missing or empty value is exit 2.
SYNC_BRANCH=none

# The name of the CI secret that holds the hub token. The name, never the value.
# One token covers every hub in HUBS, with two permissions on each:
#   Contents: read   the required checks clone the hub
#   Actions: write   the observability-signal job starts the hub's
#                    observability-gates workflow when a story merges
# No code of a pull request holds it: the story-gate-code job takes it out of
# its hub clone before it runs this pull request's code. A checks.env written
# before the rename names HUB_READ_TOKEN_SECRET, which the workflow still reads.
# A missing or empty value names HARNESS_HUB_TOKEN. Integration point: who
# issues the token, and how each new hub is granted to it, is the platform
# lead's.
HUB_TOKEN_SECRET=HARNESS_HUB_TOKEN

# The name of the CI secret that holds a read-only token for the spokes of the
# hub's list. The name, never the value. The qa-gate job reads the trunk of
# each spoke that a story depends on, through the hub's list, also when it is
# this spoke. It needs the token when a story has a blocks dependency and the
# dependency's spoke is private. The job gives git the token for the spoke URLs
# of the hub's list only. A missing or empty value names HARNESS_SPOKE_READ_TOKEN.
SPOKE_READ_TOKEN_SECRET=HARNESS_SPOKE_READ_TOKEN

# The scanner bot whose pull-request findings the harness also fixes: the
# GitHub App login of Prime Security, `prime-security-app`, when Prime reviews
# this repository's pull requests. `none` when it does not. Missing means none.
# The harness reads only the threads that this bot starts, never a person's.
# The PR review waits for the bot's review of the pushed head for at most
# PRIME_WAIT_MINUTES. The bot may not review a push after the first, so a wait
# with no review closes no finding: each one stays open until it is fixed,
# refuted, or dropped by the bot's own later review. One run fixes at most PRIME_FIX_LIMIT of the
# bot's findings, over at most PRIME_MAX_ROUNDS rounds, and at most
# HARNESS_FIX_LIMIT of the harness's own comments. Each limit is a whole number:
# PRIME_WAIT_MINUTES 1 to 120, the others 1 or more. Integration point: who
# installs the App on this repository is the security team's decision.
# PRIME_FIX_SEVERITY is the lowest rating that the run works on. Prime rates each
# finding itself: CRITICAL, HIGH, MEDIUM, LOW or INFO. A finding that Prime rates
# below this line is not fixed: the run answers in its thread with Prime's rating
# and this setting, and leaves the thread open. A missing line means LOW. A
# finding with no rating is always worked.
PRIME_BOT=none
PRIME_WAIT_MINUTES=15
PRIME_FIX_LIMIT=10
PRIME_MAX_ROUNDS=2
PRIME_FIX_SEVERITY=LOW
HARNESS_FIX_LIMIT=10

# The stack whose Prime guardrails the design writer reads, beside the core.
# `auto`, or a missing line, means Prime decides from its own summary of this
# repository: OWASP LLM for an AI component, a CIS group for a component that
# names AWS, Google Cloud or Azure. Set a list only where that summary is wrong:
# core, llm, aws, gcp, azure, joined by commas. The core (OWASP ASVS and the
# company's policies) is always read. A guardrail Prime found broken here is
# always read in full, whatever the stack. Read only when PRIME_BOT names a bot.
PRIME_STACK=auto
